How about passing the transaction object as an argument to the closure?

```
    $result = \Yii::$app->db->transaction(function ($db, $transaction) {
       $transaction->rollback();
    return 42;
    });
```

Note that you can already access the transaction object directly via $db->getTransaction().

@SamMousa, Just try it.

``` php
// Transaction::level == 0
// Transaction::begin() ==> START TRANSACTION
// Transaction::level == 1
\Yii::$app->db->transaction(function () {
    // INSERT ...
    // Transaction::begin() ==> SAVEPOINT s1
    // Transaction::level == 2
    \Yii::$app->db->transaction(function ($db) {
        // INSERT
        // Transaction::rollback() ==> ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT s1
        $db->transaction->rollback();
        // Transaction::level == 1
    });
    // Transaction::level == 1 <--- but shoud  be 2
    // Transaction::commit() ==> COMMIT
    // Transaction::level == 0
    // whole transaction already committed *errornously*
    // but it 's incomplete yet
    // INSERT ... <--- outside of true transaction
});
// Transaction::level == 0 , but shoud be 1
// Transaction::commit() ==> trying to commit outside of transaction
```

Why not just to check `Transaction::$level` like this:

``` php
// Connection::transaction()
$transaction = $this->beginTransaction($isolationLevel);
$this_level = $transaction->level;
...
while ($transaction->level >= $this_level) {
    $transaction->commit();
}
// or
$transaction->commitLevel($this_level);
```
